// The walk's binder stands for every position it will reach, because which one
// it is on is not known statically -- so a write to any position of what is
// walked is a write to a place the binder may be naming. The imprecision is in
// the place rather than in the comparison: the relation that answers this is
// the one [N-Aliased] asks between two arguments of one call, and the answer is
// settled while the program is checked rather than while it runs.
fn main() -> UInt8 {
    var xs: [UInt64; 3] = [1, 2, 3]
    var t: UInt64 = 0
    for read x in xs {
        xs[2] = 100
        t += x
    }
    UInt8(t)
}
